RT64: add GBI detection hashes for retail GoldenEye 007 (NTSC-U)

RT64 already implements GoldenEye's microcode dialect in
src/gbi/rt64_gbi_f3dgolden.cpp — F3DGOLDEN_G_TRIX at 0xB1, decoding four
triangles per command from 4-bit vertex indices. That handler is correct.

What is missing is a detection entry for the retail cartridge. The existing
F3D_GOLDEN segments are the right *lengths* (0x1420 text, 0x800 data — matching
the real ucode byte for byte) but carry hashes from a different build, and both
are marked "Needs confirmation." with the instance string
"SW Version: 2.0G, 09-30-96 (GE007)".

Hashes below were computed with XXH3-64 over the microcode extracted from a
verified NTSC-U ROM (SHA1 abe01e4aeb033b6c0836819f549c791b26cfde83), using the
same incremental scheme as GBIManager::getGBIForUCode:

    bin/gspboot.text.bin   0x1420 bytes   0xC09B06D35BC306D9
    bin/gspboot.data.bin   0x800  bytes   0xDEEF4CB184A0CAB8

Without this, getGBIForUCode falls through to
"Unable to find a matching GBI in the current database. This game is not
supported in HLE." even though the handler for it is present and working.

---

--- a/src/gbi/rt64_gbi.cpp
+++ b/src/gbi/rt64_gbi.cpp
@@
     const GBIInstance   F3D_GOLDEN                = { "SW Version: 2.0G, 09-30-96 (GE007)",        GBIUCode::F3DGOLDEN,   { false,  false,  false,  false,  false } }; // Needs confirmation.
+    const GBIInstance   F3D_GOLDEN_NTSC_U         = { "GoldenEye 007 (NTSC-U retail)",             GBIUCode::F3DGOLDEN,   { false,  false,  false,  false,  false } };

 (text segment table)
@@
             GBISegment{ 0x1420,     0xEDA47A4C2B7E69F8ULL,  { &F3D_GOLDEN } }, // Needs confirmation.
+            GBISegment{ 0x1420,     0xC09B06D35BC306D9ULL,  { &F3D_GOLDEN_NTSC_U } },

 (data segment table)
@@
             GBISegment{ 0x800,      0xB2152361A81ED3B0ULL,  { &F3D_GOLDEN } }, // Needs confirmation.
+            GBISegment{ 0x800,      0xDEEF4CB184A0CAB8ULL,  { &F3D_GOLDEN_NTSC_U } },

---

Applying this by hand is three lines. It is written as a description rather than
a strict diff because RT64's table shifts between revisions and a line-number
patch would rot immediately.

ALTERNATIVE, if you would rather not carry a fork: bypass detection entirely and
select GBIUCode::F3DGOLDEN directly. A native port always knows which game it
is, so hashing the microcode to discover it is ceremony. Doing that also removes
the requirement to place the microcode blobs in RDRAM at all — which is
otherwise a strange obligation for a port where the RSP does not exist.

VERIFYING: RT64 prints the hash it computed whenever a table entry uses
GBI_UNKNOWN_HASH_NUMB, and deduceGBIInformation() runs on a detection miss. If
you are on a different region or revision, run once and read the printed values
rather than trusting the ones above.
